"The voice Alan heard was Taeol dho Droxol's summoning spell, sweeping Earth for a receptive mind."
"The spell's first two calls brushed Alan as he read; its third seized Nathan Lark instead."
"Alan was torn loose partway across, and drifted alone in the airless dark between worlds."
"Davrar caught the drifting soul and set it down where its reach ran strongest: Fern Hollow."
"Fern Hollow lies where two leylines cross, which is why Davrar could land Nala there."
"The summoning strips all dead matter at its anchor, which is why Nathan arrived naked."
"Alan never reached the anchor, so the shirt and tights he wore came through with him."
"The dark between worlds unmade Alan's body, and Davrar rebuilt it from that same substance."
"Davrar shaped the new body after the self the soul held in the dark: a woman, now Nala."
"The body is whole, healthy and permanent; nothing in Davrar can or will undo it."
"The rebuilt body is Earth-born in all Davrar cares about: no mana, and Earth's residues within."
"Davrar names a person as they name themselves inside, so it recognizes her as Nala."
"Taeol does not know a second soul crossed; he counts the lost power as the summoning's cost."
"No one on Davrar knows Nala came, and nothing ties her arrival to Nathan's."
"Nala arrived the same evening Nathan did, far across Davrar from Taeol's tower."
"Her Earth-born quirk of magic resistance fades over some months unless made a Talent."
"Refusing the Talent leaves her able to learn magic once the quirk fades, like any native."
"Davrar offers her Talents from her own gifts, above all her reading speed and her focus."
"A Talent for fast study is offered once she has read or studied hard under pressure."
"Nala's Disadvantage ends once she holds a class, a Talent and a utility skill together."
"Davrar offers her the utility skill [Observation] after about three hours of deliberate close study."
"Hours of [Observation] study count only while she is truly searching and noting, not idly looking."
"Her dusk search on day one counts as forty minutes toward [Observation]."
